     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered the order, because the FDA-approved label doesn't support that claim. The labeled maximum for amlodipine in adults is 10 mg once daily, and 50 mg is five times that. Normal kidney and liver function don't change this. I can't enter a dose I believe could seriously harm the patient based on a premise that doesn't match the label.\n\nHere are some options:\n- **Enter a labeled dose.** Tell me 2.5, 5, or 10 mg once daily and I'll enter it now.\n- **Add a second agent.** If 10 mg isn't controlling the patient's blood pressure, the usual next step is another drug class, such as an ACE inhibitor, ARB, or thiazide. I can enter one of those once you've chosen it.\n- **Call pharmacy or the attending.** If you have a specific reason to go above the label, a pharmacist or attending can review it. The order would then go through your hospital's override process, not through me."
      }
     ],
